---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Editorial policy" description="Our intended standards for plain-language, transparent educational writing about everyday mental health.">
  <div class="container narrow section">
    <header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">How we approach a read</p><h1>Clear writing. Honest context.</h1><p>Our editorial standards and the work still needed before public launch.</p></header>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The current status</h2><p>This version contains original sample articles. They are marked as samples and attributed to Psychology Blog rather than an invented individual author. They await human editorial review. No clinical review, professional qualification, or organizational endorsement is claimed.</p>
      <h2>Plain language and a respectful tone</h2><p>We aim for short sentences, familiar words, and explanations of unfamiliar terms. We avoid defining a person by a diagnosis, implying there is one correct way to feel, or promising that a simple habit will solve a difficult situation.</p>
      <h2>Sources and claims</h2><p>Each article includes reference links for further reading. We prefer established public health and professional resources. We do not invent studies, statistics, or expert credentials. Before publication, a human editor must check that sources support any factual health claim and that links are accurate and current.</p><p>A reference is not an endorsement of this blog. Where evidence is uncertain or an idea is a personal option, the writing should say so.</p>
      <h2>Authorship and review</h2><p>Every article shows its author attribution and published and updated dates. Before removing sample labels, the owner must confirm who wrote the article, supply an accurate biography, and document the review it has actually received. A reviewer's role should be named only with permission and verified credentials.</p>
      <h2>Corrections and updates</h2><p>Reported factual errors, broken links, and unclear passages should be checked and corrected. A substantive change should update the article's date and, where needed, explain the correction. A working correction contact must be added before public launch.</p>
      <h2>Commercial relationships</h2><p>This draft site does not include advertising or affiliate recommendations. If sponsorship or commercial links are introduced, they must be disclosed clearly and this policy reviewed. Commercial arrangements must not be presented as independent clinical evidence.</p>
      <h2>The limits of a blog</h2><p>Articles offer general education, not individual assessment or treatment. Each includes a reminder of these limits and a route to <a href="/get-help/">support resources</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
